@Coruscant11, @HendrikPetertje I stand corrected, Kiro ACP does indeed expose reasoning effort control (must have been a new addition because last time I checked it didn't), so great news. 

opencode-kiro v0.2.0 and kiro-acp-ai-provider 2.1.0 add support for it and have been added to the PR. From opencode you'll be able to change it with `ctrl + t`. 

One thing that I've noticed is that the "Default" reasoning level doesn't necessarily mean off, but rather what kiro-cli uses as the model's default setting (i.e. with Opus 4.8 it's xhigh).

@m13v's point about persistent team state being more recovery code than orchestration is the right lesson, and it generalises. the durable bit you actually want isnt a team table, its liveness reconciliation, so a crashed member gets evicted instead of leaving the team wedged.

i went ephemeral-by-default in [repowire](https://github.com/prassanna-ravishankar/repowire) for the same reason. peers register on start, and a lazy-repair pass evicts ghosts when a request next needs the registry, so theres no team state to recover and nothing runs on a timer. spawn a peer, it does the task, it falls off the mesh when it dies.

not native opencode, but the teardown model youre describing (created per-task, destroyed when done, no DB tables) is exactly what fell out of building it, happy to share what worked if useful.
